  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWater vapor loop shows a fairly nice shortwave moving through\nsouthern Saskatchewan currently, and that will skirt along the \ninternational border and down into northern MN tonight. Surface \ntrough extends from southern Manitoba into western ND, with some\nshowers and thunderstorms trying to develop along it. \nDiscussion about these storms in the section below. Upper \nridging builds into the Rockies and western Plains over the \nweekend, and many of the model solutions have a ridge riding \nshortwave coming over the area Saturday night and Sunday. \nNorthwesterly flow aloft to ridging continues into the upcoming\nwork week, with several weak shortwaves moving through. \nTemperatures continue to look very warm through the upcoming \nweek, with some signs of heights starting to drop on Friday.\n\n...Severe chances this afternoon and evening...\n\nOut ahead of the surface trough/frontal boundary and across most\nof our forecast area, temperatures have rising into the 80s with\ndew points in the low 70s. Readings a little lower in our far\neast where stratus has taken its time scattering out. Sounding\nat BIS at 18Z has plenty of warm air at 850mb and still plenty\nof CIN. There is also some dry air aloft that could get\nentrained into the developing storms. All this to say,\nconvection from near the international border down into central\nND has struggled a bit to get going. We will continue to see\ndestabilization for the next few hours, and the shortwave over\nsouthern Saskatchewan will only get closer and provide upper\nsupport for convective initiation. HRRR starts developing some \nfairly intense convection around 23Z, and other CAMs are a bit \nslower or faster, but fairly similar. HREF updraft helicity \ntracks show plenty of stronger storms developing, and \nprobabilities of wind and hail from the SSCRAM are over 25 \npercent in parts of northwestern MN. If anything becomes surface\nbase instead of elevated, can't rule out some tornadoes with \nthe Nadocast and SSCRAM both showing some 10 percent chances \nalong the boundary. Most of the activity should shift eastward \nand out of the forecast area shortly after midnight as the \nshortwave moves through and we lose daytime heating. \n\n...Heat impacts this weekend...\n\nRidging continues to build over the next few days, with 850mb\ntemps near 30 C nudging into southern ND. Some high level smoke\nmight keep temps down a few degrees, and with a shortwave coming\nover the top of the ridge Saturday night into Sunday, can't rule\nout some clouds that could also keep temperatures down in a bit.\nHowever, the probability of maximum temperatures over 90 is 90\npercent or greater for all but our far northern and eastern tier\nboth Saturday and Sunday. Apparent temperatures get to 100\ndegrees in portions of east central and southeastern ND and west\ncentral MN tomorrow, with even more widespread heat index values\nabove criteria on Sunday. There is about a 40 percent chance for\nheat index values over 105 Sunday, and wet bulb globe\ntemperatures get above 80 in many areas. Not confident enough \nfor a warning at this point but could see some areas approaching\nit on Sunday. Have a heat advisory for a limited area tomorrow \nand across all but the Devils Lake Basin and Lake of the Woods \narea on Sunday.",
